'''viritoot†''', ''n.'' An unexplained word found in the following passage:
{{smaller block|<poem>What eyleth yow? Som gay gerl, God it woot,
Hath brought yow thus upon the ''viritoot''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 584.}}}}
{{smaller block|[The word is variously spelled ''viritoot'', ''vyritote'', ''veritote'', ''verytrot'', ''merytot''. Compare it with the word ''viritrate''.]}}

'''viritrate†''', ''n.'' An opprobrious term, as yet not satisfactorily explained, found in the following passage:
{{smaller block|<poem>This somonour clappeth at the wydowes gate:
“Com out,” quod he, “thou olde ''virytrate''.”</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Friar’s Tale, l. 284.}}}}
{{smaller block|[The MSS. read ''virytrate'', ''viritrate'', ''veritrate'', ''verye crate'', ''viritate'', ''veritate'', ''very trate''. Tyrwhitt has the reading ''thou olde very trate'', based upon two MSS., and regards ''trate'' as used for ‘trot,’ a common term for an old woman. The explanation is not satisfactory.]}}

'''virmilion†''', ''n.'' and ''a.'' An old spelling of ''vermilion''.

'''virolai†''', ''n.'' Same as ''virelay''.

'''virola-tallow''' (vir′ō-lä-tal″ō), ''n.'' A concrete fat from the seeds of ''Myristica'' (''Virola'') ''sebifera''.

'''virole''' (vi-rōl′), ''n.'' [< OF. ''virol'', ''virole'', also ''vireulle'', ''viroeule'', F. ''virole'', a ring, ferrule, < ML. ''virola'', a ring, bracelet, equiv. to L. ''viriola'', a bracelet, dim. of ''viria'', a bracelet, armlet: see ''ferrule''<sup>2</sup>, which is a doublet of ''virole''.] A circlet or little hoop of iron put round the end of a cane, a knife-handle, and the like; a ferrule;

