¶
'''virtually''' (vėr′tū̮-al-i), ''adv.'' In a virtual manner; in principle, or in effect, if not in actuality.
{{smaller block|They ''virtually'' deprived the church of every power and privilege which, as a simple society, she had a claim to.
{{right|''Warburton'', Divine Legation, iv., Ded.}}
The Lords of Articles . . . were ''virtually'' nominated by himself; yet even the Lords of Articles proved refractory.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., vi.}}
Weight, mobility, inertia, cohesion are universally recognized — are ''virtually'', if not scientifically, understood to be essential attributes of matter.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 507.}}
Though it was obvious that the war north of the Alps was ''virtually'' over, yet Prussia was still pouring troops into Austrian territory.
{{right|''E. Dicey'', Victor Emmanuel, p. 292.}}}}

'''virtuate†''' (vėr′tū̮-āt), ''v. t.'' [< ''virtue'' + ''-ate''<sup>2</sup>.] To make efficacious.
{{smaller block|Potable gold should be endued with a capacity of being assimilated to the innate heat and radical moisture, or at least ''virtuated'' with a power of generating the said essentials.
{{right|''Harvey''.}}}}

'''virtue''' (vėr′tū̮), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''vertue''; < ME. ''vertu'' (pl. ''vertues'', ''vertus'', ''vertuz'', ''vertous'', ''vertuis''), < OF. ''vertu'', F. ''vertu'' = Sp. ''virtud'' = Pg. ''virtude'' = It. ''vertù'', ''virtù'', < L. ''virtus'' (''virtut-''), the qualities of a man, strength, courage, bravery, capacity, worth, manliness, applied to physical and intellectual excellence; also of moral excellence, virtue, morality; < ''vir'', man: see ''virile''.] '''1†.''' Manly spirit; bravery; valor; daring; courage.
{{smaller block|And so much ''vertu'' was in Leodogan and his men that thei made hem remove and forsake place.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), ii. 335.}}}}
